index 01d59a0d0b5e08e0e83f07cfb44b6533d7d1af26..18b8f7b300afcf6d57d5e5ea5f8df1adc4b6cc8b 100644
--- a/docs/changelog.rst
+++ b/docs/changelog.rst
@@ -11,7 +11,7 @@ Unreleased
 
 Breaking changes:
 
-- ``table.foreign_keys`` now returns ``ForeignKey`` objects that are dataclasses rather than ``namedtuple`` instances, so they can no longer be unpacked or indexed as ``(table, column, other_table, other_column)`` tuples - access their fields by name instead. Compound (multi-column) foreign keys are now represented as a single ``ForeignKey`` with ``is_compound=True`` and populated ``columns``/``other_columns`` lists, where ``column`` and ``other_column`` are ``None``. Previously they were returned as one ``ForeignKey`` per column, misleadingly suggesting several independent foreign keys. See :ref:`upgrading_3_to_4` for details. (:issue:`594`)
+- ``table.foreign_keys`` now returns ``ForeignKey`` objects that are dataclasses rather than ``namedtuple`` instances, so they can no longer be unpacked or indexed as ``(table, column, other_table, other_column)`` tuples - access their fields by name instead. Compound (multi-column) foreign keys are now represented as a single ``ForeignKey`` with ``is_compound=True`` and populated ``columns``/``other_columns`` lists, where ``column`` and ``other_column`` are ``None``. The new ``on_delete`` and ``on_update`` fields expose SQLite referential actions, which are now preserved by table transforms for ordinary and compound foreign keys. Previously compound keys were returned as one ``ForeignKey`` per column, misleadingly suggesting several independent foreign keys. See :ref:`upgrading_3_to_4` for details. (:issue:`594`)
 
